<!-- ─── §01 THE PROBLEM ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Scrambled Observations, Hidden Structure</h2>

<p>The world has clean, independent structure. A robot scene has position, velocity, and lighting as separate degrees of freedom — call them latent variables <strong>z</strong>. But we never observe <strong>z</strong> directly. An unknown, highly nonlinear process <em>g</em> scrambles them into raw pixel observations <strong>x = g(z)</strong>. A camera rendering a 3D scene entangles position with lighting; sensor noise mixes velocity with texture.</p>

<p>Self-supervised learning (SSL) trains an encoder <em>f</em> that maps observations back to representations: <strong>y = f(x)</strong>. The ideal outcome is that the composed map <em>h = f ∘ g</em> undoes the scrambling — that <em>h(z) = Qz</em> for some rotation matrix Q. This is called <strong>linear identifiability</strong>: the representation is a rotation of the true latent variables.</p>

<p>Why does this matter? Because every time you evaluate a representation with linear probing, you're implicitly asking: did the model learn the true latent structure, or just some entangled approximation? Without linear identifiability, linear probes cannot recover the true latent variables exactly. And without faithful recovery, a World Model cannot support reliable planning or compositional generalization.</p>

<!-- Canvas: scrambling and recovery visualization -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">world → observation → representation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">the core pipeline</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="scramble-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The canvas above shows 200 points sampled from a 2D Gaussian (left panel: clean latents z₁, z₂), scrambled by a spiral nonlinearity g (middle panel: tangled observations x), and recovered by a trained LeJEPA encoder f (right panel: clean Gaussian structure restored up to rotation). This is the paper's main claim, made precise.</p>

<p>Prior identifiability results exist for contrastive learning, ICA, and VICReg — but not for JEPAs. JEPAs prevent collapse via an explicit Gaussianity regularizer (SIGReg), which turns out to be the critical ingredient. Without a specified embedding distribution, previous proofs didn't apply. With SIGReg, the guarantee clicks into place.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §02 LeJEPA ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-lejepa" data-code="lejepa">
<div class="section-no">02 — LeJEPA</div>
<h2>The Learner: Alignment + Gaussianization</h2>

<p>LeJEPA trains a representation with two objectives working in concert. The first pulls <em>positive pairs</em> together: two views of the same content (e.g., two nearby video frames, two augmentations of the same image) should map to nearby embeddings. The second pushes the embedding distribution toward an isotropic Gaussian — this is <em>SIGReg</em> (Sketched Isotropic Gaussian Regularization), which prevents the representation from collapsing to a constant.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">LeJEPA Objective</div>
  min<sub>h</sub> <span class="math-var">L(h)</span> = E[‖h(z′) − h(z)‖²]  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← alignment: pull positive pairs together</span><br>
  subject to: &nbsp;<span class="math-var">h(z)</span> ∼ N(0, I<sub>n</sub>)  &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← SIGReg: keep embedding distribution Gaussian</span><br><br>
  Positive pairs generated by OU transition:<br>
  <span class="math-var">z′</span> = ρ · <span class="math-var">z</span> + √(1 − ρ²) · η, &nbsp; η ∼ N(0, I), η ⊥ z &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← ρ ∈ (0,1) controls view correlation</span>
</div>

<p>The Ornstein–Uhlenbeck (OU) transition is the unique additive-noise perturbation that preserves a Gaussian marginal. Two video frames from the same scene satisfy this approximately — they share the same marginal but are correlated by ρ. The correlation ρ is the paper's key variable: high ρ means views are very similar (nearly the same frame), low ρ means views are quite different.</p>

