</section>

<!-- ─── §03 SPECTRAL KEY ───────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-spectral" data-code="spectral">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Spectral Key</div>
<h2>Why Nonlinearity Hurts Correlation</h2>

<p>The proof turns on a beautiful spectral fact about Gaussian variables. For any function h<sub>i</sub>(z) with zero mean and unit variance, we can decompose it into a <em>Hermite polynomial series</em>: a linear part (degree 1), a quadratic part (degree 2), and so on. Each degree-d component has a variance fraction w<sub>d</sub> summing to 1.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Hermite Spectral Decomposition</div>
  <span class="math-var">E[hᵢ(z′) hᵢ(z)]</span> = w₁·ρ + w₂·ρ² + w₃·ρ³ + ⋯ &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← each nonlinear degree is penalized more</span><br>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; ≤ ρ &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← because ρ² &lt; ρ for ρ ∈ (0,1)</span><br>
  with equality iff <span class="math-var">w₁ = 1</span> &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← i.e., hᵢ is purely linear</span>
</div>

<p>Since ρ ∈ (0,1), higher-degree terms like ρ² and ρ³ are strictly smaller than ρ. Every nonlinear distortion in the representation leaks variance into these higher degrees, reducing the correlation between positive pairs. <strong>The maximally correlated representation is the linear one.</strong></p>

<!-- Interactive canvas: show how nonlinearity reduces correlation -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">nonlinearity penalty — Hermite decomposition</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a ρ value to see the spectral breakdown</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="rho-controls">
      <span class="rho-label">ρ =</span>
      <button class="rho-btn active" data-rho="0.95">0.95</button>
      <button class="rho-btn" data-rho="0.80">0.80</button>
      <button class="rho-btn" data-rho="0.60">0.60</button>
      <button class="rho-btn" data-rho="0.30">0.30</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="hermite-canvas" style="width:100%;height:220px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The bars show the correlation <em>contribution</em> from each Hermite degree (degree 1 = linear, degree 2 = quadratic, etc.) for a hypothetical representation that mixes linear and nonlinear components. The red dashed line shows the maximum achievable correlation ρ (pure linear). Every nonlinear component pulls the total below the maximum. The interactive ρ control shows that this penalty sharpens as ρ approaches 1 — at high correlations, any nonlinearity is maximally costly.</p>

<p>This spectral view is also why <em>only</em> the Gaussian enables linear identifiability (Theorem 2). For a general latent distribution, the optimal "slow features" are the eigenfunctions of a Sturm–Liouville equation. These are always monotonic (giving identifiability up to a monotone transformation), but linear identifiability requires the first eigenfunction to be affine. Only the Gaussian satisfies this — its eigenfunctions are Hermite polynomials, where the degree-1 eigenfunction is exactly the identity.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─── §04 MAIN THEOREMS ───────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-theorem" data-code="theorem">
<div class="section-no">04 — Main Theorems</div>
<h2>The If-and-Only-If of World Model Recovery</h2>

<p>The paper proves four theorems that together give a complete picture. Theorems 1 and 2 form an if-and-only-if: Gaussian latents enable linear identifiability, and only Gaussian latents do. Theorem 3 shows the guarantee degrades gracefully in practice. Theorem 4 shows identifiable representations enable optimal planning.</p>

<h3>Theorem 1: LeJEPA recovers the World Model (forward direction)</h3>
<p>In a Gaussian world, any measurable map h with h(z) ∼ N(0, I) that minimizes the alignment loss must satisfy h(z) = Qz for some orthogonal matrix Q. The representation is a rotation of the true latents — and the transition dynamics are also correctly recovered: h(z′) | h(z) ∼ N(ρ·h(z), (1−ρ²)·I).</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Theorem 1 — Forward (proof sketch)</div>
  Alignment = 2n − 2 · Σᵢ <span class="math-var">E[hᵢ(z′) hᵢ(z)]</span><br>
  Spectral bound: <span class="math-var">E[hᵢ(z′) hᵢ(z)]</span> ≤ ρ &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← Hermite decomposition (§03)</span><br>
  Minimum achieved iff all wₐ₌₁ = 1 &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← all components are linear</span><br>
  ⇒ h(z) = Qz, Q orthogonal &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← forced by Gaussianity constraint</span>
</div>

<h3>Theorem 2: The Gaussian is unique (converse direction)</h3>
<p>Among all worlds with independent, stationary, additive-noise latents, only Gaussian latents guarantee linear identifiability. For any non-Gaussian distribution, there exist representations satisfying the LeJEPA objectives that are not linear in the latents.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Theorem 2 — Converse (proof sketch)</div>
  Linear identifiability ⇒ first eigenfunction φ₁ is affine<br>
  Sturm–Liouville equation for φ₁ collapses to:<br>
  &nbsp;&nbsp;(log p)′ = linear in z &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← constant score function</span><br>
  Solving: log p(z) ∝ −(z − μ)² &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← unique solution is Gaussian</span>
</div>

<!-- Before/after contrast visualization -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">latent distribution sweep — Gaussian is unique</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a distribution to see recovery quality</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="rho-controls">
