<p>The visualization shows 200 points from the given latent distribution (left: true latents z), scrambled by a sinusoidal mixing g (middle: observations), and recovered by LeJEPA (right: learned representation). For Gaussian latents, recovery is near-perfect (R² &gt; 0.999). For non-Gaussian latents, the representation is at best monotonically distorted — not linearly identified.</p>

<h3>Theorem 3: Approximate identifiability</h3>
<p>In practice the objectives are only approximately satisfied. The recovery error is bounded by the alignment gap δ (how far training loss is from its minimum) and the whitening error ε (how far the embedding covariance is from identity). Both are measurable from training, making loss a reliable proxy for identifiability.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Theorem 3 — Approximate Recovery Error</div>
  Let D = δ / (2ρ(1−ρ)) &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← nonlinearity term from alignment gap δ</span><br>
  Then ∃ Q ∈ O(n) such that:<br>
  <span class="math-var">E[‖h(z) − Qz‖²]</span> ≤ D + (ε + D)² &nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment">← vanishes when δ=ε=0 (exact case)</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─── §05 SCALING ────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-scaling" data-code="scaling">
<div class="section-no">05 — Scaling</div>
<h2>R² &gt; 0.999 Up to 1024 Dimensions</h2>

<p>Theory guarantees hold at any dimension, but can optimizers find the solution in practice? The paper sweeps latent dimension from N=2 to N=1024, using a RealNVP mixing function with a matched encoder architecture. Three Gaussianity-enforcing objectives are compared: SIGReg (explicit Gaussian regularizer), VICReg (covariance regularization), and InfoNCE (contrastive learning with implicit Gaussian).</p>

