<p>The OU-trained encoder achieves control cost ≈1.11, statistically indistinguishable from the oracle (1.04). The RL-trained encoder, whose representation is not linearly identifiable (R²=0.44), inflates cost to 2.38 — a 2× degradation. The key practical implication: <em>for self-supervised pretraining, exploration data that approximates an isotropic random walk keeps the data in the regime the theory covers</em>. Goal-directed policy data collapses onto a low-entropy region, breaking the Gaussian assumption and hurting downstream planning.</p>

<p>Theorem 3's approximate bound is validated across all runs: the measured recovery error E[‖h(z)−Qz‖²] consistently lies below the predicted bound D + (ε+D)², confirming that training loss is a reliable proxy for identifiability even in approximate regimes.</p>
</section>

